From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] a focus change that asks for a layout has to get one

Leaving a dialog that was in immersive mode leaves both system bars hidden for
good. The policy already knows they should be back; nobody ever acts on it.

Measured on V82 with WeRead's 用户协议 dialog, which is its own window
(TYPE_APPLICATION, FLAG_FULLSCREEN, vsysui=0x1706 = IMMERSIVE_STICKY |
HIDE_NAVIGATION | FULLSCREEN). Dismissing it leaves:

    mLastSystemUiFlags=0xa708            no HIDE_NAVIGATION, no FULLSCREEN
    BarController.StatusBar       mState=WINDOW_STATE_HIDDEN
    BarController.NavigationBar   mState=WINDOW_STATE_HIDDEN
    mDock=(0,0)-(1072,1448)              the bar's 90 px are not reserved
    mTopIsFullscreen=true                stale: set while the dialog was up
    mLayoutNeeded=true                   recorded, never performed

and it stays that way -- checked to 14 s, and in practice until something else
moves the focus. One traversal that changes nothing repairs all of it:

    adb shell wm overscan 0,0,0,0        the value it already had
    mDock=(0,45)-(1072,1358), both bars WINDOW_STATE_SHOWING

What the reader sees meanwhile is not an empty strip. WeRead's window carries
FLAG_DRAWS_SYSTEM_BAR_BACKGROUNDS and is still told the stable inset is 90 px,
so its own decor paints the navigation bar's background colour there: a solid
#212832 band where Back, Home and Recents were, and nothing on this device
answers a tap in it. screencap confirms which window paints it -- the band is
in the captured frame while the NavigationBar surface reads shown=false. PW3
has no navigation keys, so until the app is switched the only way out of the
application is a swipe from the bottom edge that nothing on screen mentions.

The sequence. WindowState.removeIfPossible() takes the dialog's window away,
and inside removeImmediately() the cleanup runs a surface placement there and
then:

    if (dc != null && !mWindowPlacerLocked.isInLayout()) {
        dc.assignWindowLayers(true /* setLayoutNeeded */);
        mWindowPlacerLocked.performSurfacePlacement();

That is the last placement of the transition, and it runs while mCurrentFocus
is still the window being removed, so PhoneWindowManager.beginLayoutLw() lays
the screen out from the dialog's flags: bars hidden, mDock the whole display.
Only afterwards does removeIfPossible() reach

    mService.updateFocusedWindowLocked(UPDATE_FOCUS_NORMAL, true);

where focusChangedLw() recomputes mLastSystemUiFlags from the window
underneath and returns FINISH_LAYOUT_REDO_LAYOUT, which says a layout owes to
be done with the new flags. Here that answer is written down and dropped:

    if ((focusChanged & FINISH_LAYOUT_REDO_LAYOUT) != 0) {
        displayContent.setLayoutNeeded();
        if (mode == UPDATE_FOCUS_PLACING_SURFACES) {
            displayContent.performLayout(true, updateInputWindows);
        }
    }

setLayoutNeeded() records a debt. It schedules nothing. In the one mode that
does the layout here we are already inside a placement; in the other three --
and UPDATE_FOCUS_NORMAL is the one the removal uses -- the debt stays on the
books, and whether it is ever paid depends on some later, unrelated event
causing a traversal.

Whether other devices are saved by such an event, and by which, is not
something this port has measured. What is measured is that on this one nothing
follows: the dialog's window is gone, its activity does not lay out again, and
the two candidates that would be easy to blame are both ruled out.

  - Not the animations. All three scales are 0 here (0002-global-animation-
    defaults), so a removal is never deferred for an exit animation and no
    animation frame brings a traversal with it. Setting all three to 1.0 and
    repeating the dismissal reproduces it unchanged.
  - Not the policy's own reckoning. Driving the same flags through
    PolicyControl instead (settings put global policy_control
    immersive.full=...) hides the bars and brings them back correctly, with
    mLayoutNeeded=false throughout -- no window is added or removed there and
    the focus never moves, so the placement that follows is the one that reads
    the new flags.

So the fix is to ask for the traversal in the modes where this method does not
perform the layout itself. requestTraversal() posts once; performSurfacePlacement()
clears mTraversalScheduled and removes the posted callback before it runs, so a
caller that places surfaces of its own absorbs the request rather than adding a
second pass to it.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/WindowManagerService.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/WindowManagerService.java
@@ -6041,6 +6041,19 @@
                 displayContent.setLayoutNeeded();
                 if (mode == UPDATE_FOCUS_PLACING_SURFACES) {
                     displayContent.performLayout(true /*initial*/, updateInputWindows);
+                } else {
+                    // PW3: setLayoutNeeded() records that a layout is owed and
+                    // schedules nothing. The other three modes leave it to the
+                    // caller, and the caller is not always coming back: the
+                    // removal path (WindowState.removeIfPossible) has already
+                    // placed surfaces by the time it changes the focus here, so
+                    // without this the debt stands and the screen keeps the
+                    // system bars of the window that has just gone away.
+                    // requestTraversal() posts once, and a placement that was
+                    // going to happen anyway removes that post before it runs,
+                    // so a caller that does its own absorbs this instead of
+                    // paying for a second pass.
+                    mWindowPlacerLocked.requestTraversal();
                 }
             }
 
